A mutex guarantees exactly one goroutine in the critical section:

```go
var (
    counter int
    mu      sync.Mutex
)

func increment() {
    mu.Lock()
    counter++
    mu.Unlock()
}

// 100 goroutines → counter exactly 100 (verified with mutex)
// 100 goroutines without → ~90 (verified — lost updates)
```

Without the mutex: counter++ is three operations (read, add, write); goroutines interleave and updates vanish. With: exactly 100, every time.

The rules: defer mu.Unlock() right after Lock (runs on every exit path); var mu sync.Mutex is ready (zero-value, no constructor); don't copy a mutex (go vet's copylocks catches it).

The mistake to name: incrementing without the lock because it's just one line — one line is three operations, and the update vanishes silently.
